 },
 "critic": "Nearly every card reads as generated because the deck is built from roughly twenty fixed sentence frames filled from a tiny name pool (Alex/Jordan/Maria/Yui/Kenji/Haruto/Dmitri x Hale/Novak/Reyes/Sato/Tanaka/Mori). Over twenty cards share the single 'Friday fee the Claws collect' frame word for word, and several cards are exact duplicates. Further cues: full names are repeated where a person would use a pronoun ('Walk with Kenji Tanaka... Collect it from Maria Hale'); location slots draw from a short list ('near a bar off the market', 'the ripperdoc', 'the metro entrance'); a 'role' slot is bolted on ('a labourer', 'a club worker') and has no effect on the sentence; the same first name lands on both sides ('Kenji Mori... to Kenji Tanaka', 'Yui Tanaka... Yui Mori found out first'); the odd frame 'X found out first' doesn't quite parse; and bounties come in clean tiers of 250/500/1000. The 'Kabuki:' headline frames read like a news-feed register with no local voice. The fee card is the strongest frame: it gives a clear cause (Tyger Claws protection money), a clear ask, and it sounds like Kabuki. The rumour-style lines ('Word at the stalls', 'Heard it from three people', 'put Jordan Hale on the pavement') have the most voice and causality, though they still draw on the same name pool. Accident and NCPD-pickup cards give the least reason and the least to act on."
